      "record": "18-24",
      "detail": "4th in AL East",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "Heartbreak clings to the Rogers Centre like damp spring fog - the Blue Jays clawing back from a 5-0 hole with five seventh-inning runs only to <strong>collapse 6-7 in 10 innings to the Rays</strong> on Tuesday, their third straight gut punch that leaves them 18-24, 10.5 games back in the AL East basement. Tampa Bay, scorching at 18-24 with their 16th win in 18, toyed with Toronto's bullpen late; Taylor Walls' RBI single and Jonathan Aranda's sac fly snuffed out the rally, dropping the Jays to 0-5 against these Rays this season. Rookie Fisher tasted defeat for the first time after a sterling 9-1 mark, his debut nerves exposed in extra frames. This isn't contender fire - it's a three-game skid signaling deeper rot, the bats erupting too late against a Rays staff that bent but never broke. No major injuries reported, but the urgency mounts with Vladimir Guerrero Jr. needing to ignite amid the slump. <strong> Tonight's rubber match versus Tampa at 6:07 PM ET looms large - win it, or watch the division gap yawn wider</strong>, testing if Toronto can salvage pride before the schedule turns brutal.",
        "updated": "Updated May 13, 2026"
      },
